{"name":"Design Delight","hex":"#a47bac","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"design","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A specification of an object or process, referring to requirements to be satisfied and thus conditions to be met for them to solve a problem.","A plan (with more or less detail) for the structure and functions of an artifact, building or system."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To plan and carry out (a picture, work of art, construction etc.).","To plan (to do something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/design"},{"term":"delight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Joy; pleasure.","Something that gives great joy or pleasure."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To give delight to; to affect with great pleasure; to please highly.","To have or take great pleasure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/delight"}],"entries":[{"title":"Design","blocks":[{"text":"A design is the concept or proposal for an object, process, or system. The word design refers to something that is or has been intentionally created by a thinking agent, and is sometimes used to refer to the inherent nature of something – its design. The verb to design expresses the process of developing a design. In some cases, the direct construction of an object without an explicit prior plan may also be considered to be a design, such as in arts and crafts. A design is expected to have a purpose within a specific context, typically aiming to satisfy certain goals and constraints while taking into account aesthetic, functional and experiential considerations. Traditional examples of designs are architectural and engineering drawings, circuit diagrams, sewing patterns, and less tangible artefacts such as business process models."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Braun_ABW30_%28schwarz%29.jpg/330px-Braun_ABW30_%28schwarz%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumb